There are a variety of ways, but probably the easiest is to just return a `Microsoft.Extensions.AI.DataContent`, e.g.
```C#
[McpServerTool]
public static DataContent GetMyImage()
{
    byte[] bytes = File.ReadAllBytes("path/to/my/image.png");
    return new DataContent(bytes, "image/png");
}
```

I think better option is env element than arguments. So, in the client code, you can do something like this

```csharp
var client = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(new()
{
    Id = "everything",
    Name = "Everything",
    TransportType = TransportTypes.StdIo,
    TransportOptions = new()
    {
        ["command"] = "dotnet",
        ["arguments"] = @"EveryThingServer.dll",
        ["env:TOKEN"] = "THIS IS THE TOKEN FROM CLIENT"
    }
}, new()
{
    ClientInfo = new()
    {
        Name = "Everything Client",
        Version = "1.0.0",
    }
});
```

And in Server you will be able to access it via `Environment` methods, like this.

```csharp
var token = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("TOKEN");
```
